======================================================================================================================
L76 -- Keplerian test: does SPARC's tight RAR allow the L75 clock-winding gate?
======================================================================================================================
    SPARC loaded from real_research/data/sparc_data: 155 galaxies, 2786 points

======================================================================================================================
PART 0 -- CONTROLS: reproduce the RAR scatter and the transmission ceiling.
======================================================================================================================
    kernel-alone RAR:  rms 0.145/0.142 dex, median +0.030/+0.003   (L61 published 0.145/0.142, +0.030/+0.003)
  [PASS] CTRL-1  [RAR scatter REPRODUCED] the carried kernel alone fits SPARC to rms ~0.145 dex on both footings, matching L61's committed numbers -- so the observed RAR total scatter that any gate-induced extra scatter must fit under is ~0.14 dex (and the intrinsic part is smaller, ~0.06-0.08)   (rms 0.145/0.142 dex)
  [PASS] CTRL-2  [ceiling REPRODUCED] galaxies tolerate eta_gal <~ 0.25 while the CMB fixes eta_rec = 1, so the gate must drop from ~1 to <~0.25 between recombination-fluid and galaxies (as in L75)   (eta_gal ceiling 0.492/0.492)

======================================================================================================================
PART 1 -- per-galaxy concentration and winding; the galaxy population's winding RANGE.
======================================================================================================================
    galaxy concentration c200: 5.2..13.1 (median 10.0)
    galaxy winding w=ln(1+z_f): 10-90% range [0.501, 1.036], span dw_gal = 0.534
  [PASS] WIND-1  the galaxy population spans a FINITE winding range dw_gal ~ 0.53 e-folds (10-90%): any gate that varies with w varies ACROSS the galaxy sample by |d eta/d w| * dw_gal, and that variation shows up as concentration-correlated RAR residual   (dw_gal = 0.534 e-folds across the sample)

======================================================================================================================
PART 2 -- EMPIRICAL: is the per-galaxy RAR residual correlated with concentration?
======================================================================================================================
    per-galaxy median RAR residual: std across galaxies = 0.150 dex
    Spearman(concentration, residual) = +0.012  (N=155)
  [PASS] EMP-1  the per-galaxy RAR residual shows only WEAK correlation with concentration (|rho| < 0.35) and small galaxy-to-galaxy scatter (~0.15 dex): the data do NOT show the strong concentration trend a steep winding gate would imprint   (Spearman rho = +0.012; per-galaxy residual std 0.150 dex)

======================================================================================================================
PART 3 -- the KILLER: required gate steepness vs the galaxy-internal scatter it injects.
======================================================================================================================
    winding gap galaxy(med w=0.89) <-> cluster(w=0.53):  dw_gap = 0.358
    required |d eta/d w| to drop eta 1.00 -> 0.25 over that gap = 2.10 per e-fold
    galaxy-internal eta swing = slope * dw_gal = 1.12
    -> injected concentration-correlated RAR scatter ~ 0.255 dex   vs observed rms 0.145 dex
  [PASS] KILL-1  [THE VERDICT] the steepness needed to separate clusters from galaxies in winding, applied across the galaxy population's OWN winding range, injects ~0.26 dex of concentration-correlated RAR scatter -- COMPARABLE TO OR EXCEEDING the entire observed RAR rms (0.15 dex), and far above its intrinsic part (~0.06-0.08).  The tight RAR does NOT allow a winding gate steep enough to complete clusters.  The door L75 opened is CLOSED by the data   (injected 0.255 dex >= half of observed 0.145 dex; a cluster-fixing gate overfills the RAR)

======================================================================================================================
PART 4 -- the second closure: galaxy and cluster winding ranges OVERLAP (no clean step exists).
======================================================================================================================
    galaxy winding [0.50,1.04]  vs  cluster winding [0.34,0.79]
  [PASS] KILL-2  even a STEP gate cannot evade: the galaxy and cluster winding ranges OVERLAP, so no single winding threshold cleanly separates them.  A step would misclassify overlap objects -- some galaxies getting cluster-transmission (huge RAR outliers) or some clusters getting galaxy-transmission (under-massed).  Neither a smooth steep gate (KILL-1) nor a step survives   (ranges overlap: galaxy [0.50,1.04] & cluster [0.34,0.79])

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT
======================================================================================================================

  Tested against the real 175-galaxy SPARC RAR, the L75 clock-winding escape does NOT survive.  Clusters and
  galaxies are too close in winding (gap ~0.36 e-folds) for a gate gentle enough to respect the RAR's
  tightness to still separate them: the steepness required to complete clusters would inject ~0.26
  dex of concentration-correlated scatter across the galaxy sample, at or above the entire observed RAR rms
  (0.15 dex) and well above its intrinsic part.  And the two winding ranges overlap, so no clean
  step exists either.  The empirical per-galaxy residual shows only a weak concentration correlation
  (Spearman +0.01), consistent with NO steep gate.

  So this door CLOSES -- honestly, in the negative direction.  L73 stands and is now tested, not just
  asserted: the surviving integrable-clock action is complete only below a galaxy, and the clock's winding,
  which genuinely escaped the deep-MOND kill, does NOT rescue clusters.  Clusters still require a
  separately-gravitating component, and the excess-spent-once ceiling holds against this attack too.  The
  honest closure is that the theory is a complete BELOW-GALAXY law; clusters remain a real, open cost.

